{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-adventure/1553656-league-of-extraordinary-kids","id":1553656,"title":"League of Extraordinary Kids","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Adventure!"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-09-05T19:00:24-07:00","id":1553656,"title":"League of Extraordinary Kids","content":"Try this idea, using <b>Adventure!</b> rules, recreate various kids from pulp fiction, comic strips and film. Picture Dorothy (film Dorothy but add book elements), Little Orphan Annie, Nancy Drew, and the Hardy Boys on an Adventure. Maybe the Dover Boys (an actual series of books satirized by Warner Brothers cartoons) have gone evil and our kids must stop them.<br /> <br /> Which kids from the books, comic strips and films of the 20s and 30s are you putting in your <b>League of Extraordinary Kids!</b>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-06T04:08:17-07:00","id":1553663,"title":"","content":"Tom Swift.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-06T06:32:55-07:00","id":1553667,"title":"","content":"Hmm. \ud83e\udd14<br /> <br /> I would probably prefer to do original characters clearly based on famous ones, just to avoid various problems of continuity, retcons, and sliding time scales. The Hardy Boys and Nancy Drew, for example, debuted as 16 &amp; 15 and 16, respectively before being retroactively aged up by two years a few years later and then stayed that age for decades. But that said, there are a bunch of really good ones to draw from, either directly or as inspiration. Just a few:<br /> <br /> <b>Nancy Drew: </b>Debuting in 1930 as a 16-year-old high school graduate, the only daughter of a widowed attorney from the Midwestern town of River Hights. (Much later books would place it near Chicago.) Immaculate and self-possessed, she is a student of psychology, a skilled driver, boater, and horse rider, an excellent swimmer, tennis player, and golfer, as well as dancer, seamstress, bridge player, and cook. She's also skilled in first aid and able to handle firearms.<br /> <br /> <b>Tintin: </b>Debuting in 1929 as a (perpetual) 15-ish roving reporter from Brussels, Belgium. A world traveler, skilled detective, and master of disguise, he's also an excellent athlete and capable engineer/scientist, as well as seemingly able to quickly grasp how to operate any vehicle he comes across.<br /> <br /> <b>Little Orphan Annie:</b> Debuting in 1924 as a ten-year-old orphan who gets adopted by self-made millionaire &quot;Daddy&quot; Warbucks. She and her dog, Sandy, have a wide range of adventures. These are mostly grounded if pulpish until the mid-30s, when they take on more supernatural elements. Annie allegedly ages at a quarter of the rate of other people because she was born on Leap Day (which if true could mean she was born in 1884), but this is more likely just a joke about sliding time scales. Annie's main attributes are being plucky and brave, as well as something of a trouble magnet. For this, I'd probably age the character up a little, and have her have undergone some training from her foster father's bodyguards Punjab and The Asp, and possibly even some mystical instruction from the immortal sage Mr. Am.<br /> <br /> <b>Korak the Killer:</b> Debuting in 1914 as an infant, John Clayton III, is the son of Tarzan. Living his first ten years in England, circumstances see him flee to east Africa and live the next several years in the wilds among the same sentient apes who raised his father. With him is <i>Meriem</i>, a girl he rescues from Arab slave traders who joins him in the wilds and is a proper jungle girl in her own right.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-06T09:18:34-07:00","id":1553671,"title":"","content":"This is a cool idea. I've toyed with youth pulp adventurers for Adventure! Likewise using Nancy Drew, Hardy Boys, Tom Swift and the like as archtypes.<br /> <br /> It also reminded me of the old TV show, The Young Indiana Jones Chronciles. In one episode a teenage Indiana Jones teams up with the daughter of Edward Stratemeyer, the author who created Nancy Drew, The Hardy Boys, and Tom Swift. In the episode, the daughter was based on Nancy Drew and it was essentially a teenage Indiana Jones team up with Nancy Drew. It was fun episode. That's kinda how I might imagine a young pulp adventure game for Adventure!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">Organizer for the Dead Gamers Society: The So Cal World of Darkness Meetup. </span><a href=\"http://www.meetup.com/Dead-Gamers-Society/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.meetup.com/Dead-Gamers-Society/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/85-drumheller","name":"Drumheller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar85_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-07T16:27:00-07:00","id":1553688,"title":"","content":"Characters like Tom Swift and Young Indiana Jones are perfect examples of why I prefer using archetypes rather than actual characters, as a lot of their adventures take place before the Adventure! period, and them being adults in the 1930s (Tom Swift even getting married in 1929). That said, I think you could do something really cool with young characters in the pre-WW1 period, perhaps using a mix of basic Trinity Continuum and Adventure!'s Daredevils (and perhaps a few holdovers from \u00c6ther).<br /> A ten- to fourteen-year-old Indiana Jones, Tom Swift at the start of his adventures, the Rover Boys, and any others you could find.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-09-10T23:29:30-07:00","id":1553744,"title":"","content":"Another possibly as either a character or inspiration is <b>Llana of Gathol</b>, the Martian-born granddaughter of John Carter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}